From c736041214c101a5577fc94f4b647d45b8b9474f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: E-Vex <raiedaleve@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 08:30:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] docs(registers): A-127 and DEBT-018 - the honest tier
 semantics, the isolation-is-not-recovery rule, and the counts catch up

A-127 (ASSUMPTIONS) records the isolation contract: catch_unwind is
observability, never recovery; the exit code stays non-zero;
AssertUnwindSafe's soundness argument; the panic hook's stderr stays
on as the debugging evidence; a panic=abort profile would make the
isolation a no-op by construction (none exists in this workspace);
and the tier semantics - release soaks observe panics/stuck/
resolution/throughput, only dev-profile soaks observe A12 violations,
which the nightly's soak-dev tier now sweeps over 16 rotating seeds a
night. DEBT-018 registers a found-but-deliberately-not-fixed
robustness gap from the same investigation: ReplayFile::decode
pre-allocates from untrusted length prefixes (a crafted valid-
checksum file can OOM-abort the process instead of erroring) - out of
the declared threat model (SECURITY.md: no untrusted input in the
Alpha), repay-when replays are ever exchanged between parties. The
handoff (verification commands, tools inventory, the new gotcha
section), ARCHITECTURE's tools paragraph, the CHANGELOG entry, and
the test counts everywhere (553 dev / 548 release, from 546/541) all
catch up to the code.
